research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-2543-    },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json:2544:    "thm-root-test": {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-2545-      "citations": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-2546-        {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-2547-          "fact": "L1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-2548-          "source": "lem-extended-reals-complete",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-2549-          "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-2550-          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ be **any** subset of the extended real\nline ([[def-extended-reals]]) and write $A_{\\mathbb{R}} := A \\cap \\mathbb{R}$.\nThen $A$ has a least upper bound and a greatest lower bound in\n$\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ ([[def-upper-bound]]), each unique, which we write\n$\\sup A$ and $\\inf A$ with the ambient set always $\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$.\nExplicitly:\n\n- $\\sup A = +\\infty$ if $+\\infty \\in A$, or if $A_{\\mathbb{R}}$ is not bounded above in $\\mathbb{R}$;\n- $\\sup A = -\\infty$ if $+\\infty \\notin A$ and $A_{\\mathbb{R}} = \\emptyset$;\n- $\\sup A$ is the real supremum $\\sup A_{\\mathbb{R}}$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) if $+\\infty \\notin A$ and $A_{\\mathbb{R}}$ is nonempty and bounded above in $\\mathbb{R}$;\n\nand dually, with $-\\infty$ and $+\\infty$ exchanged and \"above\" replaced by\n\"below\", for $\\inf A$ ([[def-infimum]], [[thm-infimum-property]]).\n\n**Agreement.** If $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is nonempty and bounded above in\n$\\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-bounded-set]]) then $\\sup A$ computed in\n$\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ is the real number $\\sup A$ of\n[[def-complete-ordered-field]]; if $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is nonempty and\nbounded below then $\\inf A$ computed in $\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ is the real\nnumber $\\inf A$ of [[thm-infimum-property]]. In particular the notation is\nunambiguous on the sets for which the real supremum and infimum are defined, and\n$\\sup \\emptyset = -\\infty$, $\\inf \\emptyset = +\\infty$ in\n$\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$.\n\n**No hypothesis is placed on $A$.** This is exactly what the real supremum cannot\ndo, and it is why every $\\limsup$ statement on this page holds for every sequence\nrather than for bounded ones only. It is also not a weakening of the discipline\nthis library keeps around suprema: the operation supplied here is a *different*\noperation, taken in a *different* ordered set, and the agreement clause records\nexactly where the two coincide.",
--
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3340-      "risk_review": {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3341-        "status": "pending",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3342-        "reviewer": "Alpha",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3343-        "notes": "Audit-Beta captured this A2 contract after reading every numbered step and every cited target section; independent risk review remains for A6."
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3344-      }
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3345-    },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json:3346:    "cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test": {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3347-      "citations": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3348-        {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3349-          "fact": "L1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3350-          "source": "thm-ratio-root-inequality",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3351-          "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json:3352:          "quote": "Let $(a_k)_{k \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence of reals ([[def-sequence]]) with\n$a_k > 0$ for every $k$. Put\n\n$$q_k := \\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k}, \\qquad r_k := a_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} \\qquad (k \\in \\mathbb{N}),$$\n\nwith roots as in [[thm-nth-roots-exist]] and [[def-rational-power]]. Then, in\n$\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ ([[def-limsup-liminf]], [[def-extended-reals]]),\n\n$$\\liminf_{k} q_k \\;\\le\\; \\liminf_{k} r_k \\;\\le\\; \\limsup_{k} r_k \\;\\le\\; \\limsup_{k} q_k .$$\n\n**The root sequence must start at index $1$, and $(r_k)$ is the shift that makes\nit a sequence on $\\mathbb{N}$.** The classical statement writes $a_n^{1/n}$, which\nis meaningful only for $n \\ge 1$, since $1/0$ is not a rational number; sequences\nhere are functions on $\\mathbb{N}$ and $\\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$\n([[def-sequence]]), so the root family is written $r_k = a_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)}$,\nwhich is $a_n^{1/n}$ reindexed by $n = k+1$. The ratio family $q_k$ needs no\nshift, and the four quantities in the display are those of the two sequences\n$(q_k)$ and $(r_k)$ exactly as written here.\n\n**This is why the root test dominates the ratio test.** If the ratios converge,\nthe outer two quantities coincide and the chain forces the roots to converge to\nthe same value; but the roots can converge when the ratios do not, and then the\nchain is strict at both ends. Both phenomena are exhibited by named examples on\nthe companion page.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3353-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3354-            "1.1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3355-            "3.1"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3356-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3357-        },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3358-        {
--
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3372-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3373-            "1.1"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3374-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3375-        },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3376-        {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3377-          "fact": "L4",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json:3378:          "source": "thm-root-test",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3379-          "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3380-          "quote": "Let $(a_k)_{k \\ge 1}$ be a family of reals from the starting index $1$\n([[def-series]]), put\n\n$$\\rho_k \\;:=\\; |a_{k+1}|^{1/(k+1)} \\qquad (k \\in \\mathbb{N}), \\qquad \\rho \\;:=\\; \\limsup_{k} \\rho_k \\;\\in\\; \\overline{\\mathbb{R}} ,$$\n\nand note that $\\rho$ exists for every such family, with no hypothesis whatever\n([[lem-limsup-exists]], [[def-limsup-liminf]]). Then:\n\n1. if $\\rho < 1$ then $\\sum_{k \\ge 1} |a_k|$ converges, and hence\n   $\\sum_{k \\ge 1} a_k$ converges as well;\n2. if $\\rho > 1$ then $\\sum_{k \\ge 1} a_k$ diverges;\n3. if $\\rho = 1$ neither conclusion follows: $\\sum_{k \\ge 1} 1/k$ diverges,\n   $\\sum_{k \\ge 1} 1/k^{2}$ converges, and both have $\\rho = 1$.\n\n**The root family is shifted, and that is forced.** The classical expression\n$|a_n|^{1/n}$ is meaningful only for $n \\ge 1$, since $1/0$ is not a rational\nnumber ([[def-rational-power]]), while sequences here are functions on\n$\\mathbb{N}$ and $\\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$. So the roots are written\n$\\rho_k = |a_{k+1}|^{1/(k+1)}$, which is $|a_n|^{1/n}$ reindexed by $n = k+1$,\nexactly the convention of [[thm-ratio-root-inequality]]. Every $\\rho_k$ is\ndefined, including where $a_{k+1} = 0$, by the supplementary clause of\n[[def-rational-power]].\n\n**What claim 1 does and does not say.** The comparison with a geometric series\ndelivers convergence of the series of *absolute values*; that\n$\\sum_{k \\ge 1} a_k$ itself converges is a separate step, and it is supplied by\n[[lem-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence]] earlier on this page. Nothing\nhere identifies the sum, and nothing here says anything about rearranging the\nseries, which is taken up later in this track.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3381-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3382-            "4.1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3383-            "4.2",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3384-            "5.1"
--
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3488-            "2.1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3489-            "L1"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3490-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3491-        },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3492-        {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3493-          "id": "step-4-1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json:3494:          "claim": "Suppose $\\limsup_k q_k < 1$. By the chain, $\\limsup_k \\rho_k \\le \\limsup_k q_k < 1$, so the root test applies to the family $(a_k)_{k \\ge 1}$ and gives convergence of $\\sum_{k \\ge 1}|a_k|$ and of $\\sum_{k \\ge 1} a_k$, hence of $\\sum |a_k|$ and of $\\sum a_k$; the ratio test gives the same conclusions. That is claim 1.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3495-          "step": "4.1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3496-          "inputs": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3497-            "3.1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3498-            "L4",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3499-            "L5",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3500-            "L6"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3501-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3502-        },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3503-        {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3504-          "id": "step-4-2",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json:3505:          "claim": "Suppose $\\liminf_k q_k > 1$. By the chain and [L2], $\\limsup_k \\rho_k \\ge \\liminf_k \\rho_k \\ge \\liminf_k q_k > 1$, so the root test gives divergence of $\\sum_{k \\ge 1} a_k$, hence of $\\sum a_k$; the ratio test gives the same conclusion. That is claim 2.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3506-          "step": "4.2",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-3507-          "inputs": [
