research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7574-          "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7575-          "quote": "Let $a, b$ be elements of a field ([[def-field]]) and let integer powers be as in\n[[def-integer-power]].\n\n1. For all $m, n \\in \\mathbb{N}$: $a^{m+n} = a^m a^n$, $(a^m)^n = a^{mn}$ and $(ab)^n = a^n b^n$.\n2. If $a \\ne 0$ then $a^n \\ne 0$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, and $a^{-m} = (a^m)^{-1}$ for every $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ ([[def-int-operations]]).\n3. If $a \\ne 0$ and $b \\ne 0$ then all three identities of claim 1 hold for all $m, n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7576-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7577-            "1.1"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7578-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7579-        },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7580-        {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7581-          "fact": "L3",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7582-          "source": "fs-ratio-and-root-limits-always-agree",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7583-          "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7584-          "quote": "**False claim:** for every sequence $(a_k)$ of reals with $a_k > 0$ for all $k$,\n\n$$\\limsup_{k} a_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} \\;=\\; \\limsup_{k} \\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k},$$\n\nthat is, the limit superior of the root sequence equals the limit superior of the\nratio sequence. (The root family is written with the shift of\n[[thm-ratio-root-inequality]], since $a_k^{1/k}$ is undefined at $k = 0$;\nclassically the claim reads $\\limsup_n a_n^{1/n} = \\limsup_n a_{n+1}/a_n$.)\n\nWhat is true is the chain\n$$\\liminf_{k} \\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k} \\;\\le\\; \\liminf_{k} a_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} \\;\\le\\; \\limsup_{k} a_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} \\;\\le\\; \\limsup_{k} \\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k}$$\nof [[thm-ratio-root-inequality]]. The claim above collapses its right-hand\ninequality to an equality, and that fails: the roots can converge while the\nratios oscillate. This is exactly why a root criterion decides cases that a ratio\ncriterion cannot.\n\nThe witness is $a_k = 2^{-k + (-1)^k}$. The computation below establishes all four\nquantities for it, namely\n$$\\liminf_{k} \\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k} = \\frac{1}{8}, \\qquad \\limsup_{k} \\frac{a_{k+1}}{a_k} = 2, \\qquad \\lim_{k} a_{k+1}^{1/(k+1)} = \\frac{1}{2},$$\nand it is recorded as a named example on the companion page.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7585-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7586-            "2.1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7587-            "2.2"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7588-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7589-        },
